---
/**
 * The map, on its own page.
 *
 * It was two thirds of the way down the front page, under the counts and above
 * the section cards — which meant the one control on this site that answers
 * "what is near me" was the thing you had to scroll past everything else to
 * reach, and on a phone most people never did.
 *
 * It is also the only page here that is a tool rather than a document, so it
 * gets a page whose whole job is holding it: no cards under it competing for
 * the scroll, and the filters are the first thing below the heading.
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import StateMap from "../components/StateMap.astro";
import FeatureIcon from "../components/FeatureIcon.astro";
import { buildMapPoints, MAP_KINDS, MAP_COUNTIES } from "../lib/map-points";

const points = buildMapPoints();

/* Counted from the markers themselves, so this can't drift from the map. */
const perKind = MAP_KINDS.map((k) => ({
  ...k,
  n: points.filter((p) => p.kind === k.key).length,
}));

const schema = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "WebPage",
    name: "Map of Delaware — every place in this guide",
    url: "https://delawarefieldguide.com/map/",
    description:
      "Every park, beach town, museum, winery and brewery in this guide, on one map of Delaware, filterable by county and by section.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Map — every place in this guide, on Delaware"
  description="Every park, dog beach, museum, winery and brewery in this guide, on one map of Delaware you can filter by county and by section. No map tiles, no trackers."
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap pt-11 pb-6">
    <p class="label m-0">Map</p>
    <h1 class="text-[2.4rem] sm:text-[3.2rem] mt-3.5 mb-0 max-w-[15ch] text-marsh">
      Everything, on the state
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-5 mb-0 text-[1.15rem] leading-[1.5] max-w-[54ch]">
      Every place in this guide that I have coordinates for — {points.length} of
      them. Start with a county, then switch off whatever you're not looking
      for. Tap a marker to see the place before you commit to the page.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap pb-8">
    <StateMap points={points} kinds={MAP_KINDS} counties={MAP_COUNTIES} startWith={["New Castle"]} heading={null} />
  </div>

  <div class="wrap pb-9">
    <h2 class="font-display text-[1.5rem] font-bold text-marsh border-b-[3px] border-tide pb-2 mb-4">
      What's on it
    </h2>
    <dl class="sign breakout grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-0 divide-x-2 divide-y-2 sm:divide-y-0 divide-tide/30">
      {
        perKind.map((k) => (
          <div class="px-4 py-4">
            <dt class="label m-0 flex items-center gap-2" style={`color:${k.color}`}>
              <FeatureIcon icon={k.key as any} class="w-[1em] h-[1em] shrink-0" />
              {k.label}
            </dt>
            <dd class="m-0 mt-1.5 font-display text-[1.6rem] font-bold text-marsh leading-none">
              {k.n}
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose-field pb-6">
    <h2>What isn't on it</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Anything I haven't pinned yet. A place with no coordinates in the data
      simply isn't here, which is why the count above is smaller than the number
      of pages on this site — it's a gap in what I've recorded, not a statement
      that the place doesn't exist. Dog parks and the events aren't mapped at
      all yet.
    </p>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      The markers are honest about their own precision. A solid marker is the
      place itself; a hollow one is the middle of the town, because I couldn't
      pin the building. At this scale a mile is about six pixels, so a
      town-center marker is right to within the size of the marker.
    </p>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      This is a drawn SVG, not a map service. No tiles are fetched from anybody
      and nothing here reports where you are. The outline is traced from real
      coordinates; the roads, the water and the county lines come from
      OpenStreetMap.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
